// D1881 — the value delivered is a checked read over a list whose element is a VALUE union.
// Its `T | null` has no niche: the read already yields the `{tag, payload}` box, with `null`
// riding as the tag, so every box-typed destination must pass it through instead of boxing it
// a second time.
//
// The read is `.get(i)`, not `.pop()`, because the three ASSIGN positions deliver the value
// twice and a `.pop()` mutates its receiver — the second delivery would be a different
// element, graded WRONG for the program's own reason. `popRefRecvSlot` serves both spellings
// from one row, so this grades the same rung; the mutating twin is the fixture
// `tests/cases/unions/pop-union-box-module-scope.vl`.
//
// The receiver is a SETUP binding rather than an inline call, because the ref-list slot the
// element kind is read from resolves off the receiver — an inline `vs().get(1)` is a different
// question (a call receiver), and the one under test is the one a program writes.
//
// The TEST discriminates the read value, so a cell that stored the box into a scalar cell, or
// boxed the box, is a WRONG or a SILENT rather than a pass.
//
// Format: scripts/capability-probes/matrix.py, whose docstring is the spec.
// @@PRELUDE@@
type V = i32 | string
function vs(): V[] { [1, 2] }
function vs2(): V[] { [3, 4] }
// @@SETUP@@
const popSrc = vs()
// @@VALUE@@
popSrc.get(1)
// @@TYPE@@
V | null
// @@TEST@@
v is i32
// @@HIT@@
print(v)
// @@MISS@@
print(0)
// @@WANT@@
2
// @@VALUE2@@
vs2().get(1)
// @@TYPE2@@
V | null
// @@PROOF2@@
if v is i32 { print(v) } else { print(0) }
// @@WANT2@@
4
